## 📌 Executive Summary

In Bangladesh, high-voltage transmission networks spanning 18,816 circuit kilometers (including 9,687 km of 132 kV lines) pass through remote rural corridors that present severe maintenance challenges[cite: 2]. Traditional manual ground patrols expose field workers to electrocution and fall hazards, while undetected hardware cracks and overgrown vegetation lead to sudden power outages[cite: 2].

This project proposes an automated, drone-based aerial inspection and AI monitoring system[cite: 2]. Using a VTOL hybrid fixed-wing UAV equipped with dual RGB and thermal sensors, the platform scans transmission corridors, automatically identifies damaged components and vegetation encroachments, and uploads geo-tagged fault data directly to a GIS dashboard[cite: 2, 3, 7]. Switching to this system reduces inspection costs by up to 60% while mitigating human risk[cite: 2].

---

## 🎯 Key Objectives

* **Automated Aerial Scanning:** Safely scan a 50 km transmission line corridor without requiring workers to manually climb high-voltage structures[cite: 3].
* **AI Hardware Fault Detection:** Achieve at least 90% accuracy in detecting damaged grid components such as cracked insulators or broken metal fittings[cite: 3].
* **AI Vegetation Risk Identification:** Identify tree branches encroaching on live power lines with at least 80% accuracy[cite: 3].
* **Efficiency & Cost Optimization:** Cut grid inspection time by 50% and reduce maintenance costs for grid utility operators[cite: 3].
* **Live GIS Dashboard:** Deliver a real-time online mapping system pinpointing exact GPS location coords for repair teams[cite: 3].

